        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Air Leakage Areas:



All Windows are very leaky and should be replaced.



Entry of dryer vent into utility room is very leaky.



Weather stripping around windows and doors.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>Add R-15 insulation to exterior of the home.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on insulation upgrades and the criteria for these products.</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>The insulation in the ceiling should be tested by a professional for asbestos. 



Recommend R-30 insulation be added to the attic.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on insulation upgrades and the criteria for these products.</ceilings>
                <windows>Upgrade windows to energy star zone 3 rated



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on windows and the criteria for these products.</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>If there are air sealing upgrades completed to the home, it will make it tighter, but it will not allow for the home to breathe as it was before. A mechanical ventilation system such as a HRV/ERV should be installed to allow the home to be properly ventilated. Not installing a system after air sealing can lead to issues with mold and mildew. 



There is no heated fresh air coming into the home, only cold air from cracks and leaks. A ventilation system will also improve the quality of air in the home.



The HRV/ERV will recovery heated exhaust air via a heat exchanger and preheat supply air with the extracted heat, making the units very efficient. 



HRV = Heat Recovery Ventilator

ERV = Enthalpy Recovery Ventilator



ERV also can control indoor humidity levels as well.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on ventilation systems and the criteria for these products.

</VentilationSystem>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
